It definitely is an afterthought, and I think I can explain why.  

There are two reasons for making +12V available on QBus SLUs.  The first,
which is the one usually given in the handbooks, is that those SLUs are
RS232/423 only, and have no support for 20mA current loop devices.  To
use such a device (an ASR33 or some DEC printers) you plug in a 20mA
converter (DLV11-K IIRC), which is a little black box about the size of a
cigarette pack, and which runs off the +12V supply from the SLU.  In this
case, you want a fuse to protect against mishap.  The second use is to
provide a pullup for one or more handshake lines, usually when connecting
non-DEC equipment, and in this case you want a resistor to limit the
current to a few milliamps.  Rather than rely on a special cable having
the correct resistor built-in, it was not unusual to replace the fuse
with a resistor, typically about 1 kilohm.  If you don't do that, and get
your DTE/DCE confused, you may blow the driver on the external device,
and/or the picofuse.  Is that what you did? :-)

I think the picofuse is a 1A rating, but if you really need to know I can
probably look it up on one set of diagrams or another tomorrow.

