power
> cord that I need to track down an original?

Short answer: yes.
Longer answer: be careful there because if the notch you cut is too
oversized then the cord will fit but exposed conductors may connect to parts
of the system that may be harmful (to you, the VAX or both :-().
But it can be done if you are careful ("Be careful with that axe Eugene",
Pink Floyd).

>
> The system seems to have no framebuffer, as it's just a server, so I'll
need to
> set up a console.  I've got enough systems laying around that I can use as
a
> terminal emulator.  I've found what I gather is the terminal port.  It
looks a
> bit like an RJ-11 phone cord style plug.  I haven't actually tried
plugging in
> a phone cord to see if it fits, but I did hold up an RJ-45 ethernet cable,
and
> saw that it wasn't a match.  What sort of cable will I need to set up my
> terminal?  Despite the fact that it looks like phone cord, I'm assuming
that
> just hooking it up to a modem on a PC and firing up terminal software will
not
> be enough to get going...  Anybody know of a convenient source for
whatever I
> need?  (I'm in denver, if that helps)

The MMJ connector is similar to a US phone jack. Perhaps
http://www.vaxarchive.org/ will help you. Otherwise the VMS FAQ
http://www.openvms.compaq.com/wizard/openvms_faq.html or else
http://anacin.nsc.vcu.edu/~jim/mvax/mvax_faq.html

> Okay, now assuming I can power the system up, and set up a terminal, and
no
> smoke pours out of the system, then what?  I gather that there is a
pre-boot
> interface of some sort, similar to the OpenBoot PROM on a SparcStation,
but I
> haven't run across a convenient quick guide to what I'll need to do to get
> going.

A pc with a decent terminal emulator (PuTTY) or hyperterm set at 9600 baud
will do (see the FAQ). The system prints:
- cpu designation and eprom version
- the MAC address of the ethernet interface
Next a count down ( from F to 0, or 40 to 0, depending on the VAX) and a >>>
prompt. Try HELP.
SHOW DEV or SHOW MEMORY will be useful.
SHOW BOOT will tell you which device it used to boot from.
SHOW HALT tells you what the VAX does after POST is completed (halt, boot
whatever)
SHOW BFLG keeps the bootflag. If it is non zero then then VAX was once part
of a cluster.
>
> Okay, now, assuming I get the system happily booted, I'll need to connect
it to
> my network.  (It's a server, after all...  no use just sitting in my
bedroom
> taking up space!).  Anybody have a good cheap source for the AUI
tranciever
> I'll need to connect it?

The 4300 has an on-board thin wire ethernet transceiver, next to the AUI
port. There's a black slide switch in between the two. The bulkhead shows
the effect, an appropriate LED shows what port is active.

> I have found them for sale for 40-50 dollars, but
> considering that my total investment in 2 SparcStations and this VAXServer
is
> only 20 dollars, I'm not looking to spend a ton to get AUI trancievers for
> them.

You might need a BNC/UTP ethernet HUB but these things are a lot cheaper
than a transceiver.

> Any help you can provide would be hugely appreciated.  I am really looking
> forward to haing my own little kremvax.  :)
>
> Also, I gather that the VaxServer 4000-300 would date from approximately
> 1990-1992.  Is this correct?  I found a web page that says 1992, but there
is a
> sticker on the bottom of the machine that says October of 1990, so I'm not
> sure...

Look at the serial no of the machine. It is coded FFywwnnnnn. The first two
letters (FF) indicate the factory, AY is Ayre in Scotland. The first digit
(y) is the year in which the machine was built. E.g. 1 indicates 1981, 1991
or 2001 and in case of a 4300 1991 is appropriate. The follwoing two digits
(ww) indicate the week of that year.

One note: a 4300 is an 8 VUPS machine. Vaxstation 4000 models 60, 90(A) and
96 are a lot faster. The 4300 is equiped with RF disks and these are more
difficult to come by than the SCSI disks that go into the VAXstations. OTOH
RF disks seem to have a  near infinite lifespan (though I saw one fail just
two weeks ago).

Hans



