How about a little HW-hacking? If you put the coil of a reed-relay
between the +5Volts-Standby and the main +5 volt supply-rail. Then when
the power comes back to the Powersupply there will come the
Standby-voltage to 5V. and the mainsupply will stay inactive, so I
expect the relay to energize. Wire the contact of the relay to the your
power-on pushbutton and this will "auto-powerup" the powersupply (I
hope...) once the PS is in the on-State both sides of the relay will
have +5V, and the relay will release. I havn't tested this, it's just a
thought. Anyway, this is a quick and dirty solution that offers maximum
simplicity and no significant energy-consumption. The drawback is the
link from the BU-supply to the mainsupply through the coil, if this
imposes a problem is to be thought of, I would need a circuit-diagram of
the PS to judge for this.

A somewhat cleaner method is using 2 Reed-relays, one between
+5V-Standby and GND and the second between +5V-Main and GND. The
NO-contact of Relay 1 goes in series with the NC contact of relay 2 and
all this is put in parallel to your power-on pushbutton again. This
proposal is technicalley cleaner as the previous one, but uses 100% more
relays, and dissipates power all the time. 

Anyway, these are just thoughts, nothing has been tested so far by me,
and anyway, do this only if you are skilled enough to know what you are
doing. Never work on the inside of the powersupply, there are lethal
voltages inside of thatone. And ofcourse whatever you do, you do it at
your own risk. 

Has anyone else already experimented into this direction?

By the way, isn't there an option for the powersuppley to go on
instantley? Technicalley, this would not be so difficult and for a
server it would make sense. But something like that will be inside the
PS...
Oh boy, here we go again: _All_aplicable_disclaimers_here_again_ (see
above and elsewhere) please don't kill yourself by working on a hot
powersuppley.

Frank


