Well i decided to have a go at installing that IBM hard drive i posted here
about. All seemed well until i tried copying some files from one of the old
hard drives to the new. I got a crc error during the copying of a large
backup file.
I initially went into setup, and saw that the new HD was already formatted
NTFS, and set up for one partition.
I left it that way, and like i said all seemed OK till i got the crc error.
I then thought maybe formatting it would be a good idea, and i got an error
message saying that the AlphaBIOS couldn't format partitions over 2GB in
size.
I left it alone, and decided maybe the backup file itself was at fault. I
was able to copy the rest of the drive i was replacing over to the new 18GB
IBM HD with no other problems.
After seeing the 2GB error in the AlphaBIOS, i in my infinite wisdom (and
knowing nothing about DEC computers) decided that a BIOS upgrade was called
for.
I found this FTP link, and downloaded the newest BIOS available for the
433A.
 ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/Alpha/firmware/v6.4/alpha433a/
The old BIOS was Version 5.29
It seemed to install fine, using the AlphaBIOS upgrade utility. Only thing
is that after i upgraded i got an error message something about the OS boot
loader not being found, and that it would perform the necessary repairs.
I clicked OK a couple times, and voila' it booted and i saw the new and old
hard drives and NT server fired right up.
During the boot process the QLogic SCSI BIOS would show the Tape Backup
Drive, and the CDROM as ATAPI devices with addresses 0 and 1.
And then the two hard drives at the same 0 and 1 SCSI addresses.
The Tape backup and CDROM are not connected to the QLogic SCSI card, and
appear to be on a standard 40 pin IDE cable attached to the motherboard.
Anyways after all was up and running the Tape backup when accessed would
cause a hardware failure error.
I decided to try changing the jumpers on the two hard drives from 0 and 1 to
2 AND 4.
Immediately after this i got the same error message about the boot loader
file, and i clicked ok twice, and this time it wouldn't boot, and i kept
getting the same error message.
I shut the power off at this point, and put the jumpers right back where
they were working previously, and now i get nothing no display, no boot. I
hear the hard drives spin up, and the tape backup reset, but that's it.
Damm what did i get myself into? This poor guys server is dead, and im up
shits creak.
I checked all power supply connections and made sure the CPU and memory were
seated. Tried pulling the SCSI card, and disconnecting all drives. Still
nothing.
This system ran and was restarted several times with the new BIOS upgrade.
How in the heck did swapping a few jumpers around on the hard drives cause
this thing to die?
I wasn't taking any serious static precautions the last time i went in to
swap the jumpers, but i really don't think that's it.
Any ideas for this desperate soul? Also any chance of slapping that QLogic
SCSI card in a IBM PC and getting it to work?






