> The Vax says no such device when I issue the mount command.

> The Vax is 4 foot high by 2 foot wide and 2.5 foot deep.  It has a
> flat slot to insert a reel to reel tape and also a cart tape drive. 
> It also has about 4 scsi drives for arount 4 gig total hd.  I think it
> is a microvax II but I will check that.  I am going from memory but I
> think she runs VMS 5 something.  It does not have a video card, just a
> console.  It is probablly a qbus.  There are 2 RA81 cables going to
> two different places (cards?).  Maybe I could try swapping cables, but
> why not ask first?

It sounds to me as if you've got a MicroVAX II in a Corporate Cabinet.  A
fairly common configuration (except for having SCSI hard drives, which I
assume are on a 3rd party controller).  If it is in fact a MV2, then you're
talking to the RA81 via a KDA50 controller.

I'm not to clear after reading the inital part of your message as to how
long it has been since you last used the VAX to speak to the RA81.  I think
you're saying that it was last working on last Friday and that you've had
several thunderstorms since then.  Was everything powered up, or not during
that time?

The first thing I'd recommend trying is to check all the connections on the
VAX and make sure that they're snug.  Check the cables on the RA81 to ensure
that they're snug as well if you haven't already.  If, after that, things
still don't work, try unplugging the cable that goes from the A port on the
RA81 to the /34, and plugging it into the spot where you've currently got
the other cable plugged into the VAX. 

If none of that works, it is probably time to see about reseating the boards
in the VAX, though I personally don't think it should be necessary.

Something else to consider, from reading your description of the system.  It
sounds as if the PDP-11/34 is the front end to some sort of instrument and
therefore a vital computer system.  However, it sounds like the VAX is
nothing more than a legacy device that you use to get data from the /34 to
other systems.  Maybe it's time to consider improving the setup.  Depending
on the OS on the /34 you've got a couple possible solutions open to you. 
You could add an ethernet controller to it, and possible transfer the data
that way.  Also you might be able to hook a PC up via a Serial line and
transfer the data via Kermit (depending on the volume of data transfered,
kermit may or may not be practical).

			Zane


