Franck Pissotte <franck-aenlever.pissotte@free.fr> wrote in message news:<3E4BDF41.ECAAAE5B@free.fr>...
> i have a streamer DEC TLZ06
> it is working fine but i search any doc about it.
> with google i only find TLZ07.
> i also want to know if i use a DDS2 cartride inside
> if i will get 2,3M instead of 2M with a DDS1
> because tape length is 120m versus 90m.

No. The January 1995 "Systems and Options Catalog" confirms Andy's
reply:

"... The high 4.0-Gbyte capacity of the TLZ06 tape drive is achieved
by a combination of a 90-meter cassette and a typical 2x data
compression. These features, combined with the Digital Data Storage
recording formats (DDS-DC) ensure that interchangability is retained
as capacity increases... Data compression is software selectable on
the TLZ06 tape drive and can be turned off to read and write to
60-meter DAT tapes for interchange with the TLZ04 tape drive...
366-Kbyte/s peak transfer rate (with data compression) for an
unattended backup of 1-Gbyte/hour..."

The December 1995 cat (I don't have intermediate ones) describes the
TLZ07, and it's only at this stage that 120m and DDS-2 get mentioned.

So the short answer seems to be that the best you can do on your TLZ06
is 4Gb on 90m DDS-1. (It's SCSI-2 BTW).

Chris


