Use the file command on /dev/nrmtoh you will probably find it is
a tar file...
I would suspect that you are not transfering to tape at all...
but rather taring the data into /dev/nrmtoh when you should be
sending it to /dev/nrmt0h....
Check your backup scripts carefully for typos..
If you find that you have been making this mistake, then the current
tapes are no-good (You havent put any data on them).
You can transfer the last good backup to tape using  the dd  command.
Then correct the backup script and all should be well.

As for the c at the begining of the ls -l output it shows a 'character
special file' which is a pseudo file used to represent a device driver
to the kernel.

Adam

PS Once you have the data on tape you can remove /dev/rmtoh file.
PPS Be sure you can see the difference between 0 (Zero) and o (Oh).




