one of the biggest reasons for slowness in the *BSD's is gcc and
the newer, posix-ized C library.
gcc's vax optimizer is actually better than the old DEC compiler,
there was a thread about this on the netbsd vax list maybe 1 or 2 years ago..
but much code has itself been bloated (the printf() for example, in
netbsd's c library is a good bit slower than that in older systems. )
most binaries tend to run at a similar speed in netbsd as they do in older
systems.. I run 4.3-tahoe on some of my vaxen because theyre the
absolute in speed for a unix on a vax, and netbsd on some when i need
comatibility with more modern stuff.. try building ssh on a 4.3-tahoe
machine! it's hard enough many times to get newer stuff to compile under
ultrix.  the BSD's _feel_ a lot slower, but in reality they aren't really.
Ultrix still has the best hardware support. NetBSD, which has the best
of the free systems, has run up against the lack of documentation, and
getting support for stuff like DSSI has been very slow going.
however, as an all-around system, NetBSD/vax is not bad. I have
not run 1.6 myself, but i run 1.3.3, 1.4.2, and 1.5, and theyre all
decent systems. (those systems i run on, respectively, a pair of uvax-IIIs,
a vs4k60 and vs3100/48, and a vax4k600. I also run 4.3-tahoe on another
4k600. that is a maniacially fast system!)
Ultrix tapes and cds can be found if you poke around the right place.
For most things, i do recommend give NetBSD a try. If something seems
to be slow or broken, _tell us_ on the list and people generally do
try to improve it all the time. I do have objections to the direction
that NetBSD is going in with 1.6 (eliminated the BSD style buffer
cache, introduction of a threaded kernel.. some other things), but even
if the features themselves I dont agree with, the implementation is
still done well- BSD is _not_ linux!
There is indeed much bloat in recent years. A lot of it is indirection
and abstraction introduced to facilitate the extensive compatibility
features that NetBSD seems to have been focusing on of late (after
more or less running out of new systems to port it to :), but luckily
much of that can be disabled. so, give it a try.

happy hacking,
Isildur
http://www.vaxpower.org/~isildur/computers.html





