First, the main logic board does have a 40-pin IDE interface on it, but I'm
almost dead certain that the Alpha XL processor card BIOS does not handle it.
The main logic board and chassis of the Alpha XL are a design shared with the
Celebris XL, which was produced in Pentium and Pentium Pro configurations.  The
Wintel BIOS supports IDE drives, limited to 528MB in capacity due to the age of
the BIOS.  All I can suggest is that you try attaching a small capacity IDE
drive to the system, and maybe the NT kernel will recognize it.  But probably
not.

Second, the main logic board supports 50-pin narrow SCSI drives.  The chassis
should have an internal SCSI cable with connectors available to attach other
drives.  Likewise, there are spare 5v-12v power connectors available from the
power supply.  Or, you could attach an external SCSI drive, making sure that the
SCSI adapter is terminated properly.  Make sure that any added SCSI drives have
unique SCSI identifiers.  The presumed single drive presently on the system
probably has SCSI ID zero.  The SCSI host adapter (controller) on the main logic
board is assigned SCSI ID 7.

Parallel port ZIP drives require software drivers which were never written for
NT 3.51, Alpha or Intel.

If you REGULARLY need to take data from this system, there are several
approaches which may work:

1. A functional network connection is still the best, if you can get that to
happen.
2. Next would be a SCSI ZIP drive.  Alpha NT 3.51 might treat the drive as
permanent rather than removable.  If so, you'd probably have to do a shutdown
and reboot.  I don't know for sure.  I never tried a ZIP on an NT 3.51 system.
3. Use either an external SCSI drive or an internal drive mounted in a removable
(but not hot swap) drive carrier.  Install a removable drive carrier in both
Alpha and target system which will receive the data.  Again, shutdown and reboot
are required on the Alpha NT system.

There is one potentially serious caveat if you absolutely cannot extract the
needed data through a network connection.  Obvious Alpha program code cannot be
used elsewhere, and the data can, because NT 3.51 supports NTFS (and maybe FAT
16?) partitions, equally readable elsewhere.  However, byte ordering of data due
to differing hardware architectures may prove to be problematic.  Intel CPUs are
use the opposite byte ordering of data compared to much of the rest of the
world, e.g. Moto 68000, PowerPC(?) etc.  Not sure whether the Alpha's byte
ordering is the same in this big endian-little endian byte ordering.

When all is said and done, you may still be best off accesing the needed data
thru a network.  Why not add another Ethernet card and create your own tiny
private network with another newer computer there?  ... Ben Myers

