news:3E3C9A85.412DAC06@ntlworld.com...
> I was primarily looking for a workstation to compile/test C programs
> on. Nothing too flashy is needed, but dual cpus would be nice as I do
> write multi-threaded code and it would be nice to use both cpus
> (although I have 4 in my Sun Ultra 80).
>
> There are for auction two machines
>
> DEC 7000 dual Alpha 533 server, 6 x 9.1 ( S1), with 2x533 MHz chips
> and also a
> DEC 5000 server, dual Alpha 533, (S5)
>
> The former has 512 Mb ram, the later 256 Mb.
>
> Can anyone tell me the difference between these machines? Is the 5000
> better than the 7000 or visa versa? What memory capacity do these
> machines have - 512 Mb is probably enough, but perhaps not 256 kb. My
> Ultra 80 has (or will have on Monday) 4 Gb.

AFAIk they both have the same processor(s). The difference is likely to be
found in the
number of PCI buses and the number of slots for each bus. The 5000 has one
combined
EISA / PCI slot. The other five slots are all PCI64.
Memory is not decisive: cheap and easily added. The 5000 has 8 dual banks
that accept
32 MB or 128 MB SIMMS.
The 7000 supports up to 4 processors, the 5000 only two.
The 7000 has 16 pci64 slots, of which three are shared EISA.

The 5000 has an internal UW SCSI bay that accomodates 7 disks (in plastic
containers).

> How would those DECs perform as workstations? I know they have slots
> for lots of disks, but really I don't need that. What operating
> systems would they run - I'm mainly interested in Unix/ GNU/Linux, but
> will consider running VMS too, as I basically want it as a test bed
> for testing multi-platform applications. I have a couple of Sun
> workstations, but no Dec Alphas.

I have a 5305 (one cpu, 512 MB) and it runs VMS very nicely indeed. It is
NOT supported
though, but for a home system that's probably not a real issue anyway. The
5305 runs Linux
(Debian R3 is available for it), NetBSD and of course NT 4. I haven't tried
anything but VMS.
The graphics performance may not be what you'd expect perhaps. The 5305
comes with a
Elsa Gloria II video card. That may suite your needs YMMV.
>
> I find it hard to find any information on those Decs. I've been to
> Compaq's web site and looked under 'retired systems' but can't find
> them listed at all. I'd like to know things like

Try this URL: http://h18002.www1.hp.com/alphaserver/archive/7300/
and http://h18002.www1.hp.com/alphaserver/archive/5300/
>
> number of memory slots - how ram must be installed.
> maximum number of cpus
> the type of Alpha chip - I've worked out there are a few of them.

They both have EV58 Alpha's.The systems have two memory banks, each bank
has 8 slots. In a slot fits either 32 MB or 128 MB. The banks must be filled
symmetrically,
highest capacity sims first. The 7000 up to 8 GB, the 5000 up to 2 GB.

> It must be said that Sun support old products a lot better than
> Compaq, by making service manuals and user manuals available for
> machines made 10 or so years ago. I can't seem to find this
> information on Dec Alphas.

The information is also available on the HP site. Check:
http://h18002.www1.hp.com/alphaserver/archive/index.html
>
> Are there any 'gotchas' to look out for when buying such a system?
> I've a reasonable knowledge of Unix (mainly Solaris) and Suns, but
> have never even seen a Dec Alpha (to my knowledge anyway).

Summarizing: the 7000 (aka the AlphaServer 4100) is a computerroom system
and
IMHO less suited for hobbyist use than a 5305 (aka as an Alpha Server 1200)
in terms
of size, mass and electricity consumption.

Go ahead and give it a go, especially with VMS (but I'm prejudiced).

Hans



